= Expulsion creates a rival on the left flank

The rebels are removed from shared structures and the immediate caucus becomes
cleaner on paper. In practice, the punishment converts an internal dispute into
a separate electoral actor with its own grievance story.

Barons gain short-term control, but labour and progressive organisers absorb
the cost. The coalition exits the month more disciplined and less cohesive.

- @december_2021_hub: Return to the December desk

@dec21_media
# prose-audit: Grade B — The ownership law, the re-run vote, the Washington intervention and the Palace's calculation are now on the page with three defensible Lewica lines; the newsroom's own staff are still described rather than heard.
title: Lex TVN reaches the Palace
subtitle: A media-ownership law aimed at one broadcaster becomes a test of the presidency
face-image: img/poland/events/tvp-headquarters.webp
new-page: true
view-if: december_2021_media_done = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.december_2021_media_done = 1;
Q.pis_poll_momentum = (Number(Q.pis_poll_momentum) || 0) - 0.35;
Q.institutional_issue_salience =
    (Number(Q.institutional_issue_salience) || 0) + 6;
Q.media_pluralism_pressure =
    (Number(Q.media_pluralism_pressure) || 0) + 20;
Q.news_headline =
    "The media-ownership bill passes on a re-run vote and reaches the President";
!}

= December 2021 — One law, one broadcaster

The bill restricts non-European ownership of Polish broadcasters. It is
general in wording and specific in effect: the only licence it endangers is
**TVN**, the largest commercial news broadcaster in the country and the one
whose investigative journalism the government most dislikes. Its owner is
American.

The parliamentary handling is its own scandal. A vote to adjourn is declared
lost, then reversed, then re-run; the chair recounts a division in front of a
chamber shouting that the count has been changed. The bill passes at the second
attempt on the last sitting day of the autumn.

Washington intervenes at a level Warsaw did not expect — the State Department,
the ambassador and senators from both parties, all pointing at the same
investment treaty. Tens of thousands demonstrate outside the Palace in a
December cold snap.

[? if president_name = "Andrzej Duda": President <span class="party-pis"><b>Andrzej Duda</b></span>, who has spent two
years being described as a notary for the party that nominated him, is being
asked in public whether he owns a veto. Nobody in the chamber knows the answer,
including the people who wrote the bill.?]
[? if president_name != "Andrzej Duda": President **[+ president_name +]** will
veto this and everybody knows it, which changes the question entirely. The
majority passed a law it cannot enact, in a manner that will be quoted for
years, purely to make the Palace do the refusing. What is actually being
decided this month is what the opposition extracts while the government spends
its authority on a certain defeat.?]

[? if opposition_infosec_committee = 1: The joint opposition committee we
convened in June already exists, which means there is one room where three
caucuses can agree a media position in an afternoon rather than a fortnight.?]
[? if dworczyk_stance = "Used the contents": Having read a hacked mailbox into
the record in June, our objection to a government deciding what the public may
read has an obvious counter waiting for it.?]

- @dec21_palace
- @dec21_media_front
- @dec21_media_ownership

@dec21_palace
title: Give the President public credit for stopping the law
subtitle: Buys a usable Palace channel and lets the President bank the democratic credit for a veto that was never really in doubt.
on-arrival: president_relation += 6; presidential_channel_open = 1; pis_relation -= 2; ko_relation += 1; media_capacity += 2; media_pluralism_pressure -= 8; institutional_trust += 3; news_headline = "A media veto opens a narrow Palace channel"

= The Palace channel opens on limited terms

[? if president_name = "Andrzej Duda": We say in public what most of the
chamber says in private: this veto is an institutional break with the
government, not a private favour, and it should be treated as the start of a
relationship rather than an accident.?]
[? if president_name != "Andrzej Duda": The veto was never in doubt, so we
spend the credit differently: the Palace is asked to say, on the record, what
standard it will apply to every future media bill — including the ones a
government we sit in might one day send it.?]

**[+ president_name +]** takes the credit and the polling that comes with it.
What we take is a telephone number that answers. Over the next eighteen months that channel is
worth more than the veto itself, and every time we use it KO reminds the public
who spent 2021 rehabilitating the President.

- @december_2021_hub: Return to the December desk

@dec21_media_front
title: Make ownership, public media and surveillance one democratic case
subtitle: The broadest frame available; every widened system critique also feeds the people who say all camps are identical.
on-arrival: media_capacity += 4; public_trust += 2; institutional_issue_salience += 4; media_pluralism_pressure += 6; pis_relation -= 4; konf_poll_momentum += 0.1; ko_relation += 2; news_headline = "Surveillance and media ownership become one democratic case"

= Two conflicts become one democratic file

We fuse the ownership bill, the state of public television and the surveillance
question into a single argument: public communication and state coercion cannot
be governed as party property.

It mobilises, and it hands the anti-system channels their favourite sentence —
that the whole political class is arguing about who owns the microphone. The
coalition gain is real; so is the spillover.

- @december_2021_hub: Return to the December desk

@dec21_media_ownership
title: Answer with a media-concentration bill of our own
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Refuses to defend one American broadcaster as though it were pluralism; it will be read as helping the government.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: No policy staff are free to draft a concentration statute this month.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; media_capacity += 3; media_pluralism_pressure += 10; public_trust += 3; institutional_trust += 4; administrative_reform_pressure += 3; ko_relation -= 4; left_media_doctrine = "Concentration limits and public-interest funding"; news_headline = "The Left answers Lex TVN with a general media-concentration bill"

= A rule that survives whoever is in power

Our draft does what the government's does not: limits concentration generally,
funds independent local journalism, and makes the broadcasting regulator's
appointments cross-party. It endangers no single licence and it would constrain
a Left government exactly as much as this one.

Nobody wants it in December. KO says we are complicating a straightforward
defence of a free press; the government says we agree with them about foreign
capital. Both quote the half they need. The text stays on the shelf, and is
taken down still usable the first month the Left has anything to do with media
policy.

- @december_2021_hub: Return to the December desk

@dec21_pegasus
# prose-audit: Grade B — Brejza, the timing against the 2019 campaign and the difference between a scandal and an evidential file are playable, with the technical route locked to the June information-security decision; the other targets remain a list.
title: The phone inside the campaign
subtitle: Pegasus was used on the opposition's 2019 campaign manager
face-image: img/poland/events/chancellery-2022.webp
new-page: true
view-if: december_2021_pegasus_done = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.december_2021_pegasus_done = 1;
Q.pis_poll_momentum = (Number(Q.pis_poll_momentum) || 0) - 0.6;
Q.institutional_issue_salience =
    (Number(Q.institutional_issue_salience) || 0) + 8;
Q.surveillance_scandal_stage = 1;
Q.institutional_trust = Math.max(
    0, (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) - 6
);
Q.news_headline =
    "Forensic researchers confirm Pegasus on the opposition campaign chief's phone";
!}

= December 2021 — Thirty-three intrusions, one campaign

A Canadian forensic laboratory confirms what the rumours had. **Krzysztof
Brejza**, a Civic Platform senator who ran the opposition's 2019 parliamentary
campaign, had military-grade spyware on his telephone: thirty-three separate
intrusions, running through the campaign period. Doctored fragments of his
private messages appeared on state television while that campaign was live.

This is not surveillance abuse in the abstract. A governing party's services
were inside the opposition's campaign management during a national election,
and material taken from it was broadcast by the state's own channel. We ran in
that election too, and we have no idea whose telephones were in our campaign.

Two other confirmed targets are already known. Nobody can say how many are not.

[? if dworczyk_stance = "Information security": Our June position was that
intercepted material is a state-security question rather than a scandal supply.
That position now has to survive being useful to us.?]
[? if dworczyk_stance = "Joint opposition committee": The joint committee we
convened in June already has three signatures on a common rule, and this is the
month it was written for.?]
[? if dworczyk_stance = "Used the contents": In June we read a hacked mailbox
into the parliamentary record. The government's spokesmen have already found
the clip.?]
[? if dworczyk_stance = "Refused the material": We refused to quote the hacked
mailbox in June and were called sanctimonious for it. This is the month that
decision starts paying.?]

- @dec21_technical
- @dec21_commission
- @dec21_own_phones

@dec21_technical
title: Demand an independent technical inquiry with proper evidential custody
subtitle: Requires an information-security standard of our own. Slower than outrage and admissible in a court afterwards.
choose-if: infosec_standard = 1
unavailable-subtitle: We have no information-security standard of our own — an evidential demand from this caucus would be a press release with technical vocabulary.
on-arrival: judicial_legitimacy += 4; constitutional_restraint += 5; ko_relation += 4; p2050_relation += 3; media_capacity += 2; institutional_trust += 5; surveillance_scandal_stage = 2; prosecution_oversight_capacity += 3; news_headline = "The opposition demands evidence before vengeance"

= Evidence becomes the opposition's discipline

Devices, logs, procurement records and a chain of custody a prosecutor can
actually use. The standard we wrote in June turns out to have been the
preparation for this, which nobody realised at the time including us.

PiS loses control of the timeline. The file grows outside anybody's message
discipline, and when a parliamentary commission finally exists to receive it,
the material is in a condition that survives a defence lawyer.

- @december_2021_hub: Return to the December desk

@dec21_commission
title: Force a Senate commission now and accept that it cannot compel a witness
subtitle: Hearings on television within weeks; a commission nobody must answer teaches the country that inquiries are theatre.
on-arrival: ko_relation += 3; media_capacity += 4; public_trust += 3; institutional_issue_salience += 4; pis_relation -= 7; surveillance_scandal_stage = 2; institutional_trust -= 2; news_headline = "A Senate commission on surveillance opens without the power to compel"

= Hearings without a subpoena

The Senate majority convenes it and the witnesses who matter decline to appear.
The hearings are still worth holding: the procurement trail is read out in
public for the first time, and Brejza gives evidence about his own campaign in
front of cameras.

What the country also learns is that a Polish parliamentary inquiry can be
ignored by anybody holding office. That lesson outlives this commission and is
waiting for the next one, whoever calls it.

- @december_2021_hub: Return to the December desk

@dec21_own_phones
title: Submit our own deputies' devices for independent examination
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Turns a rival party's grievance into a shared institutional one, at the risk of finding nothing and looking vain.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot pay for independent forensic examination of its own devices.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; media_capacity += 3; public_trust += 4; institutional_trust += 4; ko_relation += 2; surveillance_scandal_stage = 2; left_surveillance_audit = 1; infosec_standard = 1; news_headline = "Lewica submits its own deputies' phones for independent forensic examination"

= The question stops being one party's injury

We send the devices of our campaign staff and our most exposed deputies to the
same laboratory, publicly, with the result to be published whatever it says.
Two come back with indicators. Most come back clean.

The point is not the count. It is that surveillance is now something more than
a Civic Platform grievance, and that a party which has just opened its own
telephones can ask any minister to do the same without being laughed at.

- @december_2021_hub: Return to the December desk

@budget_2021
# prose-audit: Grade R — Routing entry only; the shared budget dossier owns the strategy, exact vote, Senate response and implementation record.
title: Minority government and Polish Deal
face-image: img/poland/events/sejm-voting-device.webp
new-page: true
view-if: december_2021_pps_done = 1 and december_2021_media_done = 1 and december_2021_pegasus_done = 1 and budget_2021_done = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.budget_2021_done = 1;
Q.next_budget_label = "December 2022";
Q.annual_budget_year = 2021;
Q.annual_budget_left_cabinet_authority =
    Q.left_in_government && Q.government_party == "pis" ? 1 : 0;
if (Q.annual_budget_left_cabinet_authority) {
    Q.budget = Math.max(3, Number(Q.budget) || 0);
    Q.budget_authority =
        "Third Morawiecki Cabinet — negotiated social-policy capacity";
} else {
    Q.budget = 0;
    Q.budget_authority = "Opposition — no state spending authority";
}
!}
go-to: poland_budget_2023_2026.annual_budget

@dec21_finish
title: Close 2021
view-if: december_2021_pps_done = 1 and december_2021_media_done = 1 and budget_2021_done = 1
on-arrival: december_2021_done = 1

The year ends with a budget carried by a government whose majority no longer
holds by itself, a media law that reached the Palace, and a socialist circle
inside Lewica that intends to be more than a commemoration. All three follow us
into 2022.

- @poland_hub: Begin 2022
